# f1 = madeOfTexts, f2 = madeOfTextsAndLists, f3 = madeOfListsAlone, f4 = listsAreJoinedFirst, f5 = oneOfTwoReads
# What is read under a computed key is expected to be what the program stores under such keys. Beside a text that goes into a builder, what has a text of its own is asked for and is appended as a part; whatever else arrives takes the sum as written, on an unlikely way.
# Texts alone are stored: the test asks for them,
# RUN(TEXTS): walk f1 mechanisms=all
# TEXTS: TypeTest {{*}} testedType=undefined|null|string
# TEXTS: StackStringBuilderAppendStringOrNullish
# and so it does where a text or a list is stored, as a parser of queries does for a name that comes twice. Of a word with objects and primitives the finder of expectations keeps the objects alone, which is right for a receiver; beside a text the primitives that stood beside them are asked for. The guess used to be refused outright because a list has no text of its own: the sum as written stood on the only way, made a rope in every turn, and the builder flattened it.
# RUN(BOTH): walk f2 mechanisms=all
# BOTH: TypeTest {{*}} testedType=undefined|null|string
# BOTH: StackStringBuilderAppendStringOrNullish
# Where lists alone are stored nothing that is expected has a text of its own: the guess would always fail and is not made.
# RUN(LISTS): walk f3 mechanisms=all
# LISTS: code f3:Body
# LISTS-NOT: StackStringBuilderAppendStringOrNullish
# RUN(NONE): walk f3 mechanisms=all
# NONE: code f3:Body
# NONE-NOT: StackStringBuilderAppendValue
# The same through a join: the lists are joined first and the texts come as they are,
# RUN(JOINED): walk f4 mechanisms=all
# JOINED: TypeTest {{*}} testedType=undefined|null|string
# JOINED: StackStringBuilderAppendStringOrNullish
# or one of two reads arrives.
# RUN(EITHER): walk f5 mechanisms=all
# EITHER: TypeTest {{*}} testedType=undefined|null|string
# EITHER: StackStringBuilderAppendStringOrNullish
